00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:50.630
if you decide--

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:52.570
not of this ever
happens, right, but you

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.970
start working with
an IP address,

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:57.172
and realize you should
have had a static address,

00:11:57.172 --> 00:11:58.630
and you're afraid
of losing it, you

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:01.390
can take any ephemeral
address that you get from us,

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:04.720
and just flip it over to
be static for yourselves.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:07.420
And it's important to note that
it only costs you if you don't

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:09.340
use the static IP address.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.080
What that means
is using ephemeral

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:12.190
doesn't cost anything.

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:15.610
If you're using static, and
it's attached to your machine,

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:17.360
it doesn't cost you anything.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:20.140
If you use a static one, and
you pull it off your machine,

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:21.790
and hold onto it,
which we told there

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:25.638
are plenty of valid reasons
why you would do that.

00:12:25.638 --> 00:12:27.680
It costs you when it's
not attached to a machine.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.560
And obviously,
the incentive here

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.700
is that we would prefer you
not park a whole ton of IP

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:37.450
addresses, and hasten the IPv4
apocalypse that will happen

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:40.405
any day now, as we're told.

00:12:40.405 --> 00:12:42.620
It'll probably happen
at the same time

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:46.710
the year of Linux
desktop happens.

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:49.100
So those are IP addresses.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.540
Firewall.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.383
I showed you that you can
open up HTTP or HTTPS.

00:12:53.383 --> 00:12:54.800
The firewall--
actually, I'm going

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:57.008
to switch back because it
would be nice if I actually

00:12:57.008 --> 00:12:58.710
