00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:36.360
Now let's get into some
not-so-basic stuff.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.665
I think that's the
getting started minimum

00:14:38.665 --> 00:14:41.040
that you need to kind of get
your machine up and running.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:42.498
Let's talk about
some of the things

00:14:42.498 --> 00:14:44.998
that make Compute Engine
maybe a little bit different.

00:14:44.998 --> 00:14:46.540
So first we'll start
with our images.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:48.757
Right, you can start
with args, which I listed

00:14:48.757 --> 00:14:49.840
a whole bunch of them out.

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:51.920
But you could always
customize those images,

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.260
and then save an image.

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:55.330
So you can start with
our version of Debian,

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.580
download all the
software you need on it,

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.780
get it all installed
and ready to go--

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:01.810
but maybe not put
your coat on just yet.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.710
And then create a
static image of it.

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:07.150
And then use that to build
and boot your machines.

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:09.400
So when you're building out
fleets of machines, that's

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:11.025
probably something
you need when you're

00:15:11.025 --> 00:15:13.300
using some of our other
tools like instance scripts,

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:17.910
where you're making multiple
auto-scaling machines.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:22.480
You actually have to do this
if you want consistency on all

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:25.780
the boxes as they get built.

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:28.638
One of the other things you can
do is boot from a container.

00:15:28.638 --> 00:15:31.180
There's this little switch up
here that says deploy container

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:32.470
image to the VM instance.

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:35.440
When you click on that, you
get this whole other set

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:37.060
of options.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.490
What you do is you point this
at a Docker repo someplace.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:42.490
It could be Docker's ones
you can pull down just

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:43.540
from straight Docker.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:47.110
Or you can use Google
Container Registry.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.030
Build your own images,
host them there,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:50.440
and then pull them down there.

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:53.440
It'll basically just launch
Compute Engine with container

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:56.590
OS and Docker running, and shove
your container in, and just

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:58.120
start it up.

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:00.040
But it's kind of
a quick easy way

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:04.920
to just get a container running
pretty fast on our platform.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.160
All right, this
one is really cool.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:09.470
So our Compute
Engine VMs by default

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:11.790
do what we call migration.

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:15.620
Which means that when we
do hardware or firmware

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:18.770
updates on the
machine, the hardware

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:22.460
that everything is running on,
we don't take down your VMs.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.710
What we'll do is we'll
copy the entire memory

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:26.817
space, and the entire--

00:16:26.817 --> 00:16:28.400
basically, everything
that's going on,

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.030
all the processing
that's going on,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:33.410
and move it over to another
instance seamlessly,

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:36.800
without you being
able to notice.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:40.050
And what this allows us to
do is we do maintenance.

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:43.880
You'll never see downtime
for our maintenance.

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:47.690
If there is hardware
failure of certain levels,

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:50.180
you won't see downtime.

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:52.310
And then, one of the
big pluses of this

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.140
is for security updates.

00:16:54.140 --> 00:17:00.667
There's a firmware level
vulnerability for Intel--

00:17:00.667 --> 00:17:01.250
or, I'm sorry.

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:02.458
It might not have been Intel.

00:17:02.458 --> 00:17:05.660
For a processor, we
won't name names.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:08.810
A while back, and a whole
bunch of our competitors,

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:09.619
we all had--

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:11.250
everybody across the
industry had this problem.

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:13.625
We need to update the hardware
because this is a zero day

00:17:13.625 --> 00:17:15.319
exploit that's really scary.

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:18.349
We were able to do that
without it causing any downtime

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:20.300
to the vast majority
of customers that

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:22.970
had live migration enabled.

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:26.150
Other vendors had to have
rolling downtime in order

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:27.420
to handle this.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:29.610
I'm actually going to show
a demo of this in action,

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:31.400
because one of the
things that you can do

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:34.560
is you can force a live
migration to happen.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:38.360
So I'm going to switch
over back to demo.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:43.940
And we'll see here that I have a
machine in my list of instances

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:47.060
called Migrate.

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:47.560
OK.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:48.860
And that has an IP address.

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:53.080
So let's go to
the terminal here.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:55.774
And I'm just going
to start pinging it.

00:17:55.774 --> 00:17:57.530
OK.

00:17:57.530 --> 00:18:00.320
And then, so that's pinging.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:01.940
And then in my
other window here.

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.532
There we go.

00:18:10.532 --> 00:18:11.990
I'll make this a
little bit bigger.

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:15.540
I realize you might
not be able to see it.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:18.290
There you go.

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:20.480
But you'll see there
at the G Cloud command

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:22.120
to simulate a maintenance event.

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:25.100
Simulate a maintenance
event means tell the VM

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:28.790
that it's having
hardware maintenance.

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.190
And it will do a
lot of migration.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:34.426
So I'm going to
hit migrate here.

00:18:34.426 --> 00:18:36.930
And at the same time, I'm
going to bump this up.

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:39.720
And you'll see that we're
getting really relatively

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:42.480
consistent ping times.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:46.570
Anywhere from 10
milliseconds to,

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:49.247
I've seen up to about
200 milliseconds.

00:18:49.247 --> 00:18:51.330
Some of this is obviously
dependent on the network

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:54.750
that I'm running on because
it has to go from this machine

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:55.950
to Google.

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:59.350
But we are moving this
machine right now.

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:03.960
And you'll see that there's
almost no disruption.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:06.060
Now, if you have a more
advanced service, if you--

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:07.560
like, because I'm
just doing a ping.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.950
Right, that's not that
taxing on the machine.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:17.360
What we've seen is that
you won't drop connections.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:20.270
But some connections may
take just a little bit longer

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:24.500
to answer while it's in
the process of migrating.

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:26.270
But we are still
migrating, and we

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.010
haven't seen any downtime yet.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:32.640
And we won't because we're done.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.940
And we've just moved the machine
from one piece of hardware

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.890
to another without us
seeing it surface at all

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:40.373
at the connectivity level.

00:19:40.373 --> 00:19:41.540
What you guys think of that?

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:45.040
Is that something
you guys could use?

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:45.580
No?

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:47.200
Oh, OK.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.320
All right, tough room guys.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:52.770
You just had the keynote, right?

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:57.050
All right, so I'm going to
switch back to my Preso.

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:59.655
And we'll go a little
bit deeper into stuff.

00:19:59.655 --> 00:20:01.030
All right, so some
of the options

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:04.280
that I didn't touch here is that
you absolutely can attach GPUs

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.890
to your machines.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:10.120
We have several types of GPUs
available, mostly Nvidia.

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:16.337
Tesla K80s, P100s, P4s,
T4s, and V100 GPUs.

00:20:16.337 --> 00:20:18.670
Now it's important I put this
right after live migration

00:20:18.670 --> 00:20:22.210
because these are things
that are especially

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.250
attached to the harbor
that you're running.

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:27.520
And so basically, you
can't do the live migration

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:28.410
with them running.

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:32.350
Right, if you are running GPUs,
you cannot do a live migration

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:33.460
operation.

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:35.760
Which kind of makes
some sense, right,

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:37.680
because we have to
have the hardware,

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.330
and the hardware is good.

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:43.060
We also have available
something called

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:47.800
TPUs, which are Google's
custom tensor processing

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:52.640
units that are optimized
for ML operations.

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.190
Ooh, that's a bad spelling.

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:56.673
They are now available
for preemptible pricing.

00:20:56.673 --> 00:20:58.840
And I'll talk a little bit
about preemptible pricing

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:01.140
when I get to the
pricing section.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.050
But basically, if
you have advanced--

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:06.700
or, you just have ML work,
and you want to kind of turbo

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:08.050
charge it.

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:12.055
Switching to GPUs and activating
that option in your tensor

00:21:12.055 --> 00:21:18.850
flow or Cloud MLE, or Cloud
Machine Learning Engine boxes

00:21:18.850 --> 00:21:24.600
will make those operations
faster, and ultimately cheaper.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:26.680
We're going to take
a slight detour.

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:29.350
And I want to talk about
something called Cloud Shell.

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:33.100
So a lot of times, people
set up VMs, and set them up

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:33.820
as a workstation.

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:37.210
Right, they have one systems
workstation that they setup,

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:40.270
and use that to connect to their
entire cloud infrastructure.

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:41.500
And you can do that.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:43.400
We're happy to have you do that.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:47.410
But we also have Cloud Shell
available for you, which is

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:50.470
a browser-based terminal, CLI.

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:54.190
You saw the SSH window
that we kind of give you,

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:56.200
but we also have it
kind of hooked up

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:59.980
to a custom VM that is
always available for you.

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:03.460
It has a home directory, so you
can store commonly used scripts

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:06.100
there, and have them
migrate with you

00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:09.640
as your you're working on them.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.680
But we don't charge
you anything for it,

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:13.490
so you can just always
have it available.

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:15.970
And I'm going to switch
back, and show it.

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:19.300
Just how to get to
it, and how to use it.

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:20.980
Up here at the top
of my console there's

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:23.840
this little terminal
looking thing.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:26.443
I'm going to go ahead
and activate Cloud Shell.

00:22:26.443 --> 00:22:28.360
See, it's going to
provision a machine for me.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:34.230
And I can always open
this in a new window.

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:38.403
And we'll see that we're
establishing our connection.

00:22:38.403 --> 00:22:39.820
What's great about
this is I don't

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:42.220
have to maintain any of
the software on here.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:45.840
I can use G Cloud projects.

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:49.410
Projects list.

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:50.980
And so G Cloud is
running on this.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:52.780
It's all up to date.

00:22:52.780 --> 00:22:55.270
It's all the latest version.

00:22:55.270 --> 00:22:58.060
Same thing with the
Kubernetes controls.

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:00.910
Same thing with most of
the kind of universally

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:04.010
used on Google Cloud tools.

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:07.300
They're all updated
already for you to use.

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:08.670
So I'm going to switch back.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:16.520
And, oh, let's talk
