And you'll see, I
have the entry there.

00:24:30.970 --> 00:24:33.300
But basically I
just cURLed metadata

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:37.350
dot Google internal, compute
metadata, instance name,

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:37.930
and I got--

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:42.750
where did it go?

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:45.088
It was there a second ago.

00:24:45.088 --> 00:24:45.630
There you go.

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:47.813
Delete Me, that was the
name of the machine.

00:24:47.813 --> 00:24:49.730
And I could also do that
at the project level.

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:55.820
Right, so I can go back
here, and grab this one.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:59.360
So again, a lot of times
I'm writing custom code,

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:00.670
and I don't want to know--

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:03.620
I don't want to hard code any
other project details into it.

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:05.120
I could just use
this, and get this.

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:07.370
One important thing is
that you want to always

00:25:07.370 --> 00:25:09.860
make sure you include that
header down at the end of it,

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:12.170
the metadata flavor Google.

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:14.690
Otherwise it'll return an error.

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:19.430
But basically, any of the
SDK for any of the language

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:21.508
run-times that you
might run on here

00:25:21.508 --> 00:25:23.300
usually have this built
in so they can just

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:25.050
pull this information.

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:27.980
But you can get to it directly,
and deal with it however

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:30.200
by just grabbing the URLs.

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:31.460
This is all secure.

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:36.992
So for machine metadata,
it's actually doing a process

00:25:36.992 --> 00:25:37.700
under the covers.

00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:39.530
And the know, the
request, and the data

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:42.000
never leaves the VM itself.

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:45.950
For project, I also
believe that it's

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.080
grabbing data that's locally.

00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:50.090
But I have not--

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:51.810
I'm not I'm 100%
certain of that.

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:56.480
But just be aware that you can
trust that that data is not

00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:58.970
being transmitted anywhere.

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:03.980
All right, I'm going to
switch back to Preso.

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:04.760
Metadata.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:06.135
So now, let's talk
about pricing.

00:26:06.135 --> 00:26:10.340
And like I said, pricing
is a giant hurdle,

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:13.280
I think, a lot of
times with VM projects.

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:15.380
And basically, the logic
goes something like this.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:18.860
It used to be the case
where you over-buy hardware.

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:21.110
Right, because you're going
to amortize it over three,

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:22.820
or five, or eight years.

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:26.870
And so you want it to be
able to grow to your usage

00:26:26.870 --> 00:26:29.040
three to five years from now.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:32.303
So you over-buy, which
is totally valid strategy

00:26:32.303 --> 00:26:34.220
when you're doing on-prem
stuff because you're

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:36.050
planning for growth.

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:37.730
But then you do
that same process,

00:26:37.730 --> 00:26:39.480
and you go, oh, well
I'll go to the cloud.

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:42.620
And I had, you know,
10 64 CPU boxes.

00:26:42.620 --> 00:26:45.080
I'm just going to repeat
that, and do the same thing.

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:48.050
And you get this bill
that sort of outrageous.

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:50.630
And that combined with the
fact that you're not actually

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:55.160
using all of that processing
power means that you're not

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:57.020
just overpaying, you're
overpaying for stuff

00:26:57.020 --> 00:26:58.000
that you're not using.

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:00.890
Right, so we have
a couple of things

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:06.650
to encourage strategies for
you to kind of curb your usage

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:08.690
to kind of conform to
the way that you'll

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:10.830
get the best pricing on us.

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:14.750
Wow that was a complex
sentence I just said there.

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:17.690
All right, so let's start
with the first one, which

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:19.100
is our sustained use discount.

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:21.160
So one of the--

00:27:21.160 --> 00:27:24.020
I kind of talked to that whole
process where you over-buys,

00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:25.310
and you plan for growth.

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:26.390
Well, we do that too.

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:30.980
Right, so we take on a risk, and
buy extra machines in the hopes

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:35.210
that people will use
them, and pay us for them.

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:38.060
That risk is still there, and
that's still a risk for us.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:42.800
So any time that we can push
that risk onto our customers,

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:43.820
we'll make a deal.

00:27:43.820 --> 00:27:46.250
Right, we'll lower the price
because it's good for us

00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:48.180
to share some of this risk.

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:51.087
So if you have long-running
machines, that's good for us.

00:27:51.087 --> 00:27:52.670
Because the longer
and more consistent

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:56.310
you guys are running
machines, the more easy

00:27:56.310 --> 00:27:58.060
we can plan for growth.

00:27:58.060 --> 00:27:59.660
Right, because it's
more consistent.

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:01.970
So we like regular usage.

00:28:01.970 --> 00:28:04.700
We like long-running machines.

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:06.848
So the longer you
use the machines,

00:28:06.848 --> 00:28:08.390
the longer we'll
give you a discount.

00:28:08.390 --> 00:28:12.740
So you get up to 30%
off of our list price

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:15.200
over the course of a
month for running a VM.

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:16.700
Now it's important
to note that this

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:18.950
is calculated over
all of your instances,

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:20.600
and not just one or two.

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:25.340
So for example, if you spin
up a box for half a month,

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:27.450
and then spin up another
box for half a month,

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:30.380
we'll actually combine that
into a full month of a box

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:31.852
even though they were separate.

00:28:31.852 --> 00:28:33.185
We call that inferred instances.

00:28:33.185 --> 00:28:35.360
So let me show
you how this looks

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:37.130
from a machine standpoint.

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:41.540
So day one, end of month,
I start with one machine,

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:43.520
and I run it for half the month.

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:45.560
And then I need to add
additional capacity,

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:49.760
so I'm running a second machine
also for part of the month.

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:54.320
I add a third machine for
the back half of the month.

00:28:54.320 --> 00:28:55.580
And then I add some extras.

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:58.880
Right, so I'm running
five machines total.

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:00.990
None of them are running
for a full month.

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:02.758
So you wouldn't
necessarily expect

00:29:02.758 --> 00:29:03.800
to get the full discount.

00:29:03.800 --> 00:29:07.250
But what we do is we
lump these all together.

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:10.070
Right, so we cut off five,
and split it into this.

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:12.590
And basically, we kind
of go from the start

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:14.750
of when the machine
ran the earliest,

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:16.700
and then plan out
for a whole month.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:18.200
We'll see that one
and three combine

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:19.570
to become a full machine.

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:23.510
Two, four, and five combine to
become majority of a machine.

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:25.580
And five is a partial.

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:27.350
So then we calculate
the discount

00:29:27.350 --> 00:29:29.660
by saying, OK, this
set of machines

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:33.830
that we're counting as one gets
100% of the discount, 30% off.

00:29:33.830 --> 00:29:36.360
The second one ran for
about 80% of the month,

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:38.310
so we'll give it 80%
of that discount.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:44.552
And then the fifth one ran for
just around 25% of the month.

00:29:44.552 --> 00:29:46.760
And so that's how we'll
calculate your sustained use,

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:52.000
not just on each individual
part as it relates to a month.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.880
So the more you run,
the cheaper we are,

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:59.030
and we're happy to have
your consistent usage.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:29:59.530
OK.

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:00.510
So that sustained use discount.

00:30:00.510 --> 00:30:01.620
And the great thing
about that is you

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:03.060
don't have to do anything.

00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:05.130
I mean other-- if
you want to game it,

00:30:05.130 --> 00:30:08.580
you can certainly kind
of make sure you're

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:09.900
getting the best one you want.

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:12.220
But if you run a VM, we just
apply this automatically.

00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:15.130
There's no switch you
have to flip or anything.

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:17.130
So the next pricing control
we have is something

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:19.230
called committed use discount.

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:22.260
And we fought this
for a long time

00:30:22.260 --> 00:30:27.210
because committed use struck
us as decidedly uncloud-y.

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:29.100
Right, you're taking
a machine and saying

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:31.440
I will run this machine
for three years.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.550
I'll run this
machine for one year.

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:37.320
Which basically, you just
moved your hardware commitment

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:39.852
from your data center
to us without really

00:30:39.852 --> 00:30:42.060
taking advantage of the fact
that we're on the cloud.

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:43.770
If you need more,
you can ask for more.

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:46.140
If you need less,
you can ask for less.

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:48.160
So we do it a little
bit differently.

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:50.040
You're not committing
yourself to a machine.

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:52.540
You're not saying, I
will have this one 64

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:54.930
core box for the
next three years.

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:59.880
You're paying for an allotment
of processors and RAM.

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:01.932
And we'll give you up
to 50% off depending

00:31:01.932 --> 00:31:03.390
on their configuration
and whatnot.

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:04.470
But here's the thing,
you can configure

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:07.350
this however you want, and
you can change it at any time.

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:12.275
So let's say I agree
to buy 300 20 cores.

00:31:12.275 --> 00:31:14.400
Memory would also apply,
but it would make the math

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:15.100
a lot uglier.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:16.440
And I can't do math on the fly.

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:18.660
So we'll just say,
for sake of argument,

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:20.670
this is just our
processor allocation.

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:23.070
Memory is also involved, but
we're not going to show it.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:25.470
So I have 300 20 cores.

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:28.350
I split it up into
16 20 core boxes.

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:29.010
Great.

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:31.320
Further down the road, I
figure out that, like, man,

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:34.770
I really need a
beefier machine to do

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:36.150
this one set of operations.

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:40.500
What I can do is tear
down four of the 20s,

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:41.895
and turn it into an 80.

00:31:41.895 --> 00:31:43.270
And this is still
the same thing,

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:46.140
so it's still the same discount,
still the same everything

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:48.670
because it's the same
number of processors total.

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:49.920
And I can do it the other way.

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:51.280
Right, I can go lower.

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:53.460
I can break up some of
my machines into smaller.

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:54.210
I can do whatever.

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:57.380
As long as I'm still at the
same processor allocation,

00:31:57.380 --> 00:31:59.250
the committed use
discount will apply,

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:02.036
and all of our agreements
are all in place.

00:32:02.036 --> 00:32:02.850
Which is great.

00:32:02.850 --> 00:32:06.060
Now, if you need
more, obviously, you

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.690
can do committed use along
with other strategies.

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:12.450
You can just buy an extra VM.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:14.100
But once you commit
to this, you will

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:19.870
pay for the term of your
commitment regardless.

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:22.140
So that is another way.

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:24.330
All right, so that has
been pricing tricks.

00:32:24.330 --> 00:32:27.370
And now I want to talk about
some of our usage controls.

00:32:27.370 --> 00:32:29.980
So we have something
called custom VMs.

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:31.980
I showed you, you can
dial in exactly the number

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:34.860
of processors and
memory that you wanted.

00:32:34.860 --> 00:32:37.410
And what this allows you
to do is a lot of times,

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:41.850
you look at those preset
configurations for machines.

00:32:41.850 --> 00:32:45.150
Right, you've got your
two, your four, your eight,

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:47.698
your 16, your 32, your 64.

00:32:47.698 --> 00:32:49.740
But like, you may find
that one of your processes

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:51.970
runs perfectly at 20 processors.

00:32:51.970 --> 00:32:54.030
Right, that's the optimal.

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:56.200
So you're kind of
left with a choice.

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:59.640
Do you under spec
at 16, and maybe not

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:01.860
be as performing as
you possibly can be?

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:03.510
Or do over spec at 32?

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:07.860
And just say well, I'll waste
that money because, you know,

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:08.760
I'm not paying.

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:10.830
My bosses is paying it.

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:13.650
But what you can do is dial
in exactly to 20 with us,

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:17.758
and get the exact performance
you need for as little money as

00:33:17.758 --> 00:33:19.050
possible as you can pay for it.

00:33:19.050 --> 00:33:21.450
And actually, I
have a demo of this.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:26.520
So I'm going to switch
back to the machine here.

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:28.357
And I'm going to start
this off, and then

00:33:28.357 --> 00:33:29.940
explain what I'm
doing because it does

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:32.200
take about two minutes to run.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:36.210
So I have here, you'll see, 64--

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:39.740
I'm sorry, 32
machines ready to go.

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:44.230
And I'm calling out to the APIs
to request machines for them.

00:33:44.230 --> 00:33:46.140
And you'll see that's
two, four, six.

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:50.250
It's all the permutations
of custom VMs I can use.

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:53.580
What's going to happen is these
boxes are going to spin up.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:56.400
And they're going to calculate
100 million digits of pi

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:58.543
on each box as soon
as they start up.

00:33:58.543 --> 00:34:00.210
The reason why I do
that is because that

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:03.480
is a very processor
intensive operation.

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:06.660
But the tool that
we use for crunching

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:12.030
pi, kind of at some point, you
don't get better performance

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:14.370
by adding more processors to it.

00:34:14.370 --> 00:34:17.425
And so what's going
to happen is all

00:34:17.425 --> 00:34:19.050
of these machines
are going to spin up,

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:22.139
and we'll see which one
does it the fastest.

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:26.370
And we'll also see
which one does it the,

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:29.050
like, optimal
performance for cost.

00:34:29.050 --> 00:34:31.260
So the first part of
what we're doing here

00:34:31.260 --> 00:34:33.567
was the requesting
from our data center.

00:34:33.567 --> 00:34:34.650
So basically, we say, hey.

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:35.830
We would like a VM.

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:37.747
And the data center takes
a little bit of time

00:34:37.747 --> 00:34:40.210
to figure out, do we
have capacity for this?

00:34:40.210 --> 00:34:40.710
Yeah.

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:43.060
We do, or we don't.

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:46.920
But it looks like in most
cases, we did have capacity.

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:48.550
The next thing it does is build.

00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:52.560
All right, so we're going to
take the processor and memory

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:54.659
configuration that
you asked for.

00:34:54.659 --> 00:34:57.750
Find it in the data center,
kind of lump them together,

00:34:57.750 --> 00:34:59.513
and then turn them
into a machine.

00:34:59.513 --> 00:35:01.680
After we build, we're going
to configure the network

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:06.570
to make sure that it can
talk publicly and privately.

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:08.235
And then after it
does that, it's

00:35:08.235 --> 00:35:10.110
obviously going to boot
its operating system.

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:11.490
And then the first
thing it does,

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:13.278
after it's all done
its boot operations,

00:35:13.278 --> 00:35:15.570
that it immediately calculates
100 million digits of pi

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:19.130
using y-cruncher.

00:35:19.130 --> 00:35:22.560
So we'll see this is happened.

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:23.060
We'll see.

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:24.803
I wonder which one
is going to win?

00:35:24.803 --> 00:35:26.470
I know which one is
optimal because I've

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:29.140
done this hundreds of
times, but it always

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:31.880
surprises me which one wins.

00:35:31.880 --> 00:35:35.350
There is no real
difference in terms

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:42.340
of speed of a boot process by
how you configure your machine.

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:46.870
It used to be that the
custom numbers were

00:35:46.870 --> 00:35:49.690
a little bit longer because
we already pre-allocated

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:51.880
some of the pre-set numbers.

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:54.200
That doesn't seem to
be the case anymore.

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:55.120
And so, let's see.

00:35:55.120 --> 00:35:56.170
We already have a winner.

00:35:56.170 --> 00:36:00.810
The winner was-- wow, the screen
is so big that I can't tell.

00:36:00.810 --> 00:36:02.620
I can't read it from here.

00:36:02.620 --> 00:36:04.720
Let's see.

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:07.600
So 40 processors won.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:11.820
But we're still chugging along.

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:14.337
Like I said, this usually takes
about two minutes to finish.

00:36:14.337 --> 00:36:15.920
We're just going to
let it finish out.

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:17.550
And when it's done,
what we're going to do

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:19.633
is we're going to isolate
just the pi calculation,

00:36:19.633 --> 00:36:23.840
and look at the
curve it creates.

00:36:23.840 --> 00:36:24.680
We're done that one.

00:36:24.680 --> 00:36:26.990
And that one should
be done any second.

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:29.690
So a lot of interesting
observations.

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:31.370
40 won.

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:35.300
But a lot of the
high performing ones

00:36:35.300 --> 00:36:40.140
were sort of down here in
the lower processor range.

00:36:40.140 --> 00:36:42.963
So let's isolate pi calculation.

00:36:42.963 --> 00:36:44.630
And you'll see that
we get a nice curve.

00:36:44.630 --> 00:36:47.900
Right, this is definitely
processor related.

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:49.460
But eventually it
kind of craps out,

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:51.377
and it doesn't get any
better just because you

00:36:51.377 --> 00:36:52.760
add more processors to it.

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:55.580
Having done this
hundreds of times,

00:36:55.580 --> 00:36:58.040
it's around 28
processors, is where

00:36:58.040 --> 00:37:00.170
it starts to become kind of--

00:37:00.170 --> 00:37:03.770
you're not getting benefits
out of adding more machines.

00:37:03.770 --> 00:37:05.890
But as we see, 20--

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:09.440
in terms of the total cost
of how much this costs,

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:11.390
we go from the
smallest thing we could

00:37:11.390 --> 00:37:16.720
do, a two processor box, all the
way up to a 64 processor box.

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:20.435
And our price starts at 0.2
cents for that operation,

00:37:20.435 --> 00:37:24.170
all the way up to 6.5
cents for the operation.

00:37:24.170 --> 00:37:28.520
So one, we just calculated
100 million digits of pi

00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:31.550
on multiple machines that we
spun up from nothing just right

00:37:31.550 --> 00:37:33.260
now, which is kind of cool.

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:36.770
And we were able to figure out
what the optimal processor was

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:40.423
for this, or about 28.

00:37:40.423 --> 00:37:41.090
So that's great.

00:37:41.090 --> 00:37:45.320
I'm going to switch
back to my Preso.

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:46.380
And let's see.

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:48.118
So that was custom VMs.

00:37:48.118 --> 00:37:49.910
Now let's talk about
preemptible VMs, which

00:37:49.910 --> 00:37:51.350
is another strategy we have.

00:37:51.350 --> 00:37:54.770
Preemptible VMs are 1/5th
the cost of our normal VMs.

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:58.640
So just right out the
door, just 1/5th the cost.

00:37:58.640 --> 00:38:02.112
However, they will
disappear in 24 hours.

00:38:02.112 --> 00:38:04.070
Sometime in the next 24
hours, they'll go away.

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:05.960
It could be any time
in that 24 hours,

00:38:05.960 --> 00:38:08.000
but they'll definitely
go away within 24 hours.

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:09.050
You'll lose the machine.

00:38:09.050 --> 00:38:10.940
You have about a 30
second warning before it

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:13.010
just kind of goes away.

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:17.870
But then what you can do is you
can always request another one,

00:38:17.870 --> 00:38:19.437
and we'll give it to you.

00:38:19.437 --> 00:38:20.270
So what is this for?

00:38:20.270 --> 00:38:23.240
It's not for running your
permanent, you know, e-commerce

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:25.190
or website presence.

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:27.320
It's not for anything
that's user-facing.

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:28.430
It's for bash work.

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:32.810
It's for work that you don't
care when it gets done.

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:38.360
Right, it can support having an
interruption, and restarting.

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:42.400
So cue processing, batch
work, anything like that.

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:42.900
So

00:38:42.900 --> 00:38:44.525
Let's kind of show
how this would work.

00:38:44.525 --> 00:38:46.100
So I have a machine here.

00:38:46.100 --> 00:38:48.290
This is the-- what is this?

00:38:48.290 --> 00:38:50.120
This is the micro.

00:38:50.120 --> 00:38:51.980
We're going to bump
it to the one CPU.

00:38:51.980 --> 00:38:53.810
We're going to bump it to 32.

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:56.990
And then we're going to bump
it all the way up to 96.

00:38:56.990 --> 00:38:59.540
And when we get there, we
see that the price per month

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:01.190
is $2,300.

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:05.840
As calculated, that's $3,300
for the actual full cost of it.

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:08.208
And then you're getting $1,000
off in the sustained use

00:39:08.208 --> 00:39:10.250
discount if you're running
it for the full month.

00:39:10.250 --> 00:39:12.890
And that's how we
arrive at $2,300.

00:39:12.890 --> 00:39:18.200
With preemptible VMs, what you
get is, the total cost drops--

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:20.720
sorry, the original
cost drops to $701

00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:24.260
because it's 1/5th
of that $3,500.

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:27.350
But you don't get the
sustained use discount,

00:39:27.350 --> 00:39:30.510
and then it comes out at $701.

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:32.270
So this is a really
great strategy

00:39:32.270 --> 00:39:34.190
for lowering the
cost for operations

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:36.560
that don't need to happen
at a particular time,

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:37.968
particular moment.

00:39:37.968 --> 00:39:39.010
AUDIENCE: Can you speak--

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:39.440
TERRY RYAN: The--

00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:40.510
I'm sorry, is there a question?

00:39:40.510 --> 00:39:41.354
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:39:41.354 --> 00:39:42.900
Can you speak to to the restart?

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:45.163
So you don't know when
you're going to go down?

00:39:45.163 --> 00:39:45.830
TERRY RYAN: Yes.

00:39:45.830 --> 00:39:47.863
AUDIENCE: How do you bring
yourself back up when

00:39:47.863 --> 00:39:48.723
you do it five times a day.

00:39:48.723 --> 00:39:50.196
Say you've only got
a four hour slot.

00:39:50.196 --> 00:39:51.180
How does that actually work?

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:52.680
TERRY RYAN: That's
a great question.

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:54.498
So one, you can always
just do it manually.

00:39:54.498 --> 00:39:56.540
Right, you have to check
to see if it's up, and--

00:39:56.540 --> 00:39:58.410
right, but we do
want to do that.

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:01.260
The way most of our
customers who are using this

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:03.240
as a strategy do it, they--

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:05.700
and I'm going to talk
about this right now.

00:40:05.700 --> 00:40:06.603
Instance groups.

00:40:06.603 --> 00:40:08.520
Where you can create
these groups that you say

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:11.042
have a minimum, a maximum,
and have them always running.

00:40:11.042 --> 00:40:12.500
You can set them
to be preemptible.

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:16.200
So if one of those machines goes
down, it'll start backed up.

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:19.320
And that's probably the
best strategy for doing it.

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:20.110
Yes?

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:21.790
AUDIENCE: How will
these preemptible VMs

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:23.356
be before they go?

00:40:23.356 --> 00:40:24.545
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:24.545 --> 00:40:25.170
TERRY RYAN: No.

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:28.410
So they will live at some time.

00:40:28.410 --> 00:40:31.150
They will live for
up to 24 hours.

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:33.780
So that is the
longest they'll live.

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:36.630
But they can, in theory,
shut down immediately

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:37.650
after you start them up.

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:40.530
Because what's happening
under the covers is,

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:43.088
we have machines that
aren't being used,

00:40:43.088 --> 00:40:44.880
allocated in certain
ways, and certain data

00:40:44.880 --> 00:40:45.690
centers, and whatnot.

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:47.773
And when you ask for one
of these preemptible VMs,

00:40:47.773 --> 00:40:51.180
you're saying I want it, but
I don't want it that badly.

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:55.920
Right, so what will happen
is within that 24 hours,

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:58.470
if another customer comes
along and says, no, no.

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:00.150
I need this machine
all the time.

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:02.610
And I'm going to pay
the full price for it.

00:41:02.610 --> 00:41:05.620
We say, well that's the deal
we made, and we'll take it.

00:41:05.620 --> 00:41:09.000
So that's why you don't have
any control over how long--

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:10.970
because like, maybe
no one asks for it.

00:41:10.970 --> 00:41:12.870
You get it the whole 24 hours.

00:41:12.870 --> 00:41:14.490
Maybe someone asks
for it immediately,

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:17.670
and it's taken away.

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:18.170
All right?

00:41:18.170 --> 00:41:20.253
I'm going to try to get
through this next section,

00:41:20.253 --> 00:41:22.170
and I'll leave time for
questions at the end.

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.530
So as I was saying before,
one of the things--

00:41:24.530 --> 00:41:26.040
once you want to
start scaling out,

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:28.470
once you want to start making
your operations bigger,

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:31.500
or a little bit more
self-managing, what you're

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:34.020
going to want to do is take
the next step towards managed

00:41:34.020 --> 00:41:36.120
and unmanaged instance groups.

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:39.960
These are groups of machines
that are all connected to a--

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:41.430
well, they're
groups of machines.

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:43.290
That's really all they
are under the covers.

00:41:43.290 --> 00:41:45.000
They're not that useful
unless you hook them up

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:46.375
to a load balancer,
in which case

00:41:46.375 --> 00:41:48.630
then you can have them
share responsibilities.

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:51.300
But if you have a managed
or unmanaged group.

00:41:51.300 --> 00:41:54.090
Managed groups, you have to
create an image template.

00:41:54.090 --> 00:41:58.230
So you'll create a disk
like I outlined before.

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:00.660
You'll create a machine profile.

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:02.970
And you will say this is
the template for this,

00:42:02.970 --> 00:42:09.390
and create machines in this
group to this template.

00:42:09.390 --> 00:42:11.910
Then, because they're
all exactly the same,

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:15.510
we can do things like
auto-scale and auto-heal them.

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:20.050
You can say, have the process
utilization always be 60%.

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:23.130
And so if it goes above
60%, we will spin up

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:24.720
more instances for you.

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:27.620
If it drops below 60%
over all your instances,

00:42:27.620 --> 00:42:30.690
we will spin down
one to you, for you,

00:42:30.690 --> 00:42:32.650
with you being able
to set min and max.

00:42:32.650 --> 00:42:35.400
So you may want to just
say min three all the time.

00:42:35.400 --> 00:42:39.120
Just so I have some redundancy,
and also some redundancy.

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:43.770
And then set an upper limit,
like this should never

00:42:43.770 --> 00:42:45.400
go to 20 machines.

00:42:45.400 --> 00:42:50.250
So if it does, there's a
problem, so have the max be 20.

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:53.220
Unmanaged groups are again
a collection of machines

00:42:53.220 --> 00:42:54.720
that you hook up
to a load balancer.

00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:56.880
The only difference here is
that they're heterogeneous.

00:42:56.880 --> 00:42:58.297
And because they're
heterogeneous,

00:42:58.297 --> 00:43:00.680
we really can't do
no auto nothing.

00:43:00.680 --> 00:43:03.720
Right, like we don't
know the impact

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:05.730
of adding another
machine because you

00:43:05.730 --> 00:43:08.670
have all different sets
of processors allocations.

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:09.990
That's fine.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:12.252
So you can set
these up this way.

00:43:12.252 --> 00:43:14.710
And then finally, you're going
to want to hook one of these

00:43:14.710 --> 00:43:16.320
up to our load balancers.

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:18.850
And we have a couple different
flavors of load balancer.

00:43:18.850 --> 00:43:21.040
Our HTTP HTTPS load
balancer, which

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:23.470
is sort of the equivalent of
what other people would refer

00:43:23.470 --> 00:43:25.570
to as an L7 load balancer.

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:28.300
TCP and UDP are
network load balancers

00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:30.610
that are running at L4.

00:43:30.610 --> 00:43:32.860
And then either of
these, you can set up

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:36.690
to be internal only so that it's
only routing internal traffic.

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:39.130
Now I'm going to walk through
the console of these things

00:43:39.130 --> 00:43:39.880
real quickly.

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:41.380
But I'm not going
to set anything up

00:43:41.380 --> 00:43:43.450
because it usually takes
a little bit of time.

00:43:43.450 --> 00:43:49.060
So I'm going to switch
to the demo monitor here.

00:43:49.060 --> 00:43:50.530
Oh yeah, perfect.

00:43:50.530 --> 00:43:51.580
And let's look at this.

00:43:51.580 --> 00:43:54.853
So let's see here.

00:43:54.853 --> 00:43:56.770
We're going to start
with our instance groups.

00:43:56.770 --> 00:43:59.050
Now I have an instance
group already setup.

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:01.670
You'll see that I have
three instances running.

00:44:01.670 --> 00:44:03.700
And I have a rule
that the target CPU

00:44:03.700 --> 00:44:05.630
utilization is always 60.

00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:08.560
We can go in, and we can
see this is a managed group

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:11.290
so that it will
auto-scale and all this.

00:44:11.290 --> 00:44:12.632
I have a template set up for it.

00:44:12.632 --> 00:44:13.840
I'm just going to go through.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:15.840
I'm going to mess around
with the minimums here.

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:18.280
I'm going to say a minimum
five instead of three.

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:23.100
And then I'm going to
set the maximum to 10.

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:25.930
And I'll save that.

00:44:25.930 --> 00:44:28.980
And all of this is wired
up to a load balancer.

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:32.760
So let me go here, and
we'll see the load balancer.

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:35.720
And this is pretty
basically straightforward.

00:44:35.720 --> 00:44:38.730
I get a front end, which is
my IP address, in which case

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:43.100
this is an HTTP load balancer,
so it's doing mostly HTTP.

00:44:43.100 --> 00:44:45.540
And I can set up
host paths and rules,

00:44:45.540 --> 00:44:48.330
or I can just have
it just completely

00:44:48.330 --> 00:44:50.490
load balance the whole thing.

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:52.590
But I could setup
multiple back-ends

00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:55.280
to work on different paths
on the front-end here.

00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:57.280
I have a lot of
control over that.

00:44:57.280 --> 00:44:59.280
And then down here, I
have my back-end services,

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:02.670
which was that instance group
I just showed you earlier.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:04.260
And so if I go to
this IP address,

00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:08.550
you'll see my really exciting
default Apache web page.

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:11.830
Because I was really kind of
throwing this together quickly.

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:12.610
There we go.

00:45:12.610 --> 00:45:13.660
So that's running.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:16.650
So one of those
boxes is responding.

00:45:16.650 --> 00:45:22.950
And then finally, in that
last step, I at some point

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:25.740
updated the number of machines.

00:45:25.740 --> 00:45:27.270
And we'll see that
it is currently

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:30.600
spinning up those machines.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:33.430
Basically, it's in the process
of creating them right now.

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:35.180
So that's why they're
kind of labeled red.

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:37.770
But because I said minimum
five instead of minimum three,

00:45:37.770 --> 00:45:39.553
that's why it's
spinning up more.

00:45:39.553 --> 00:45:40.970
All right, I'm
looking at my time,

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:42.450
and I'm looking at
my Preso, so I'm

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:46.930
going to switch back
to the presentation.

00:45:46.930 --> 00:45:48.000
And let's see.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:50.070
So a couple of
quick conclusions.

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:54.240
One, there are other
products in the GCP

00:45:54.240 --> 00:46:00.010
that use Compute Engine as the
underpinnings for the product.

00:46:00.010 --> 00:46:03.270
For example, Kubernetes Engine
is using straight Compute

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:05.400
Engine VMs under the covers.

00:46:05.400 --> 00:46:09.870
And same thing with Dataflow,
Dataproc, and Cloud Shell.

00:46:09.870 --> 00:46:10.763
Why is this relevant?

00:46:10.763 --> 00:46:11.680
Why is this important?

00:46:11.680 --> 00:46:13.430
Because you can use
some of the strategies

00:46:13.430 --> 00:46:15.870
you would use maintaining
Compute Engine instances

00:46:15.870 --> 00:46:16.860
with these products.

00:46:16.860 --> 00:46:22.080
For example, Kubernetes Engine
is for managing containers.

00:46:22.080 --> 00:46:26.010
You're running host nodes
that run Kubernetes on it.

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:30.150
You can do things like create
a managed instance group that

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:33.240
is made up of entirely
preemptible VMs,

00:46:33.240 --> 00:46:35.520
and have that support
your container work.

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:37.860
So you can dramatically
lower the cost

00:46:37.860 --> 00:46:39.690
of running a Kubernetes
Engine cluster

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:42.540
by moving some of the
work onto preemptible VMs

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:44.940
because you can set it up
all through Compute Engine.

00:46:44.940 --> 00:46:48.450
Same kind of strategies apply
for Dataflow and Dataproc.

00:46:48.450 --> 00:46:51.400
And Cloud Shell has access
to the metadata too.

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:52.860
So all of these things add up.

00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:55.110
You may want to look at
applying some of the stuff

00:46:55.110 --> 00:46:59.010
that we talked about here to
one of our these other products.

00:47:01.810 --> 00:47:05.410
Also, you get a free
tier of Compute Engine

00:47:05.410 --> 00:47:06.710
just by having a project.

00:47:06.710 --> 00:47:11.300
So your first F1 micro
instance is free in perpetuity.

00:47:11.300 --> 00:47:13.340
So it's not a very beefy box.

00:47:13.340 --> 00:47:15.790
It's a shared processor
with six gigs of RAM,

00:47:15.790 --> 00:47:17.770
but if you just want to
play around, and start

00:47:17.770 --> 00:47:20.930
fooling around VMs if
you haven't already,

00:47:20.930 --> 00:47:24.130
you can use that, and
kind of dip your feet in.

00:47:24.130 --> 00:47:28.240
With that, I will say this
